  "statement_token": "\"Let $A = \\\\{a_1,a_2,\\\\dots,a_n\\\\}$ be a set of positive integers with $a_1 < a_2 < \\\\dots < a_n$. A **Skolem set** is a set for which $\\\\{1,\\\\dots,2n\\\\}$ can be partitioned into pairs whose differences are the elements of $A$. **The perfect Skolem set conjecture.** The set $A$ is a Skolem set if and only if the number of even $a_i$ is even and\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\sum^n_{i=m} a_i \\\\leq n^2-(m-1)^2\\n$$\\n\\nfor each $1 \\\\leq m \\\\leq n$. This proposes that the elementary parity and density conditions are also sufficient for ordinary sets, although the corresponding existence problem for multisets is substantially harder.\"",
  "statement_token_sha256": "dc2064c8c4e3c2e7cbf005353db0285d9dcb23ec64c2662014489c93259cb49e",
  "statement": "Let $A = \\{a_1,a_2,\\dots,a_n\\}$ be a set of positive integers with $a_1 < a_2 < \\dots < a_n$. A **Skolem set** is a set for which $\\{1,\\dots,2n\\}$ can be partitioned into pairs whose differences are the elements of $A$. **The perfect Skolem set conjecture.** The set $A$ is a Skolem set if and only if the number of even $a_i$ is even and\n\n$$\n\\sum^n_{i=m} a_i \\leq n^2-(m-1)^2\n$$\n\nfor each $1 \\leq m \\leq n$. This proposes that the elementary parity and density conditions are also sufficient for ordinary sets, although the corresponding existence problem for multisets is substantially harder.",
